# parity-deriva

An event-driven lab for finding statistically interesting price patterns and,
once one looks promising, trading it for real - with a simulated twin of the
broker running alongside the live execution handler on the same event stream.
The two are compared step by step; when reality drifts too far from the model,
the divergence itself is the signal, and it can raise an alarm or stop trading.
That is where the name comes from: *parity* is the invariant you want between
the simulated and the real side, *deriva* is what you measure when it breaks.

No broker is the default one. Six are behind it - OANDA, eToro, IG,
Capital.com, Interactive Brokers and any MetaTrader 5 broker - plus a paper
session on Twelve Data's candles, and each live session picks its own broker
and account. Instruments are whatever the chosen broker offers; the work so far
has been on DE30_EUR and EUR_USD, so not only forex. Historical candles are
kept locally in HDF5, so research runs offline and needs no broker at all:
they come from CSV exports (Dukascopy's or a broker's), from another parity
server, or from any broker that serves bid and ask bars.

It began, long ago, as a fork of [QSForex](https://github.com/mhallsmoore/qsforex)
by Michael Halls-Moore. None of that code is left - the engine, the event
hierarchy, the data layer, the strategies and the execution path were all
rewritten around candles - on the OANDA v3 API first, the broker it started
with, then on the others - and moved from Python 2 to Python 3 - but the idea of an event-driven forex stack was his, and so are
the thanks.

